use /lib
from /scope/editor import EditorScope

export scope UserPreferencesScope:
    concept: {UserPreferencesConcept}

concept UserPreferencesConcept:
    pitch:
        The choices the application remembers for the user, rather than for a
        project, so each launch starts as the last one left off.

    shape: {UserPreferencesShape}

shape UserPreferencesShape:
    description:
        Preferences are remembered for the user across launches and across all
        projects, in the platform's per-user config directory, never in a
        project.

    preferences:
        - light or dark mode, which, until chosen, follows the operating system's appearance
        - the interface's brightness, one for dark mode and one for light, each starting at 0
        - the colours chosen in the theme editor, the base and Code, Chain, Spec, and Ask, for dark mode and for light, each the theme's own until chosen; restored before brightness, which they bound
        - whether the editor wraps long lines, as the @{EditorScope} says, off until turned on
        - whether the ribbon is collapsed, which starts expanded
        - the layout of a file's diff, which starts side by side
        - the recent projects
        - the folder a project was last opened from
        - the harness runs go to, which starts as Claude Code
        - whether to check for updates automatically, which starts on
        - whether to show the welcome page at launch when something needs attention, which starts on
        - whether the walkthrough has been taken or skipped, which starts as not

        A preference is saved as soon as it is chosen, and restored on the next
        launch. A brightness saved by an earlier version, in its steps of 2% of
        the way from black to white, is restored as the same brightness.

    failing:
        A preference that can't be saved still holds for the rest of the
        session. Only light or dark mode says so, as the commands that change
        it do; the others, brightness and the chosen colours among them, fail quietly, being only a
        convenience.
